    TRACE WOULD A TWO-FORWARD-SAMPLE COMPARATOR READ THE PLANE? -- `(far - near) / (far + near + guard)`, neither term the ant's own cell:
        near/far blind (both 0) along POINTED HOME     along AWAY separation
           1 / 2         4.3%        -0.0177        -0.0136    -0.0041
           1 / 3         4.1%        -0.0574        -0.0304    -0.0270
           2 / 4         4.4%        -0.0887        -0.0775    -0.0112
           3 / 6         5.6%        -0.1602        -0.2306    +0.0704
          6 / 12         8.1%        -0.2890        -0.4670    +0.1780   <- Z29's literal suggestion
      SEPARATION IS THE COLUMN. The shipped `(ahead - here)` reads -0.20 home against -0.24 away:
      a gap of 0.04 and NEGATIVE in both. A comparator earns its place by making that gap real
      AND by not being blind -- a pair that reads 0.0 most of the time is not a sensor.
      READ THE TOP ROW'S SHARE. `(AtNest, Drop, 1.0889)` against `(Bias, Drop, -0.2)` puts P(drop) at
      EXACTLY 0 anywhere below adjacency, at any crop fill. So a small top row means the ants never
      REACH the comb, and a large one with few drops would mean they reach it and decline.
